  <h1>Ozempic vs. Mounjaro in 2026: Which Should You Choose?</h1>
  <div class="article-meta"><span>Feb 24, 2026</span><span>·</span><span>18 min read</span></div>
  
<p>If you're considering a GLP-1 medication for weight management or diabetes, chances are you've narrowed it down to two names: Ozempic (semaglutide) and Mounjaro (tirzepatide). They're the two most talked-about medications in the space — and for good reason. Both deliver remarkable results, but they work differently and the right choice depends on your specific situation.</p>
<p>This guide breaks down everything you need to know to make an informed decision, based on clinical trial data, real-world experience, and practical considerations like cost and insurance.</p>

<h2>The fundamental difference</h2>
<p>The most important distinction is their mechanism of action. Ozempic (and its weight-management counterpart Wegovy) contains semaglutide, a GLP-1 receptor agonist that mimics one gut hormone. Mounjaro (and Zepbound) contains tirzepatide, a dual GIP/GLP-1 receptor agonist that mimics two gut hormones simultaneously.</p>
<p>Think of it this way: semaglutide is pulling one lever very effectively. Tirzepatide is pulling two levers at once. The additional GIP receptor activation enhances fat metabolism, improves insulin sensitivity in fat tissue, and may help explain why tirzepatide consistently shows greater weight loss in trials.</p>

<div class="callout"><strong>Quick summary:</strong> Ozempic = one hormone (GLP-1). Mounjaro = two hormones (GLP-1 + GIP). Both are weekly injections. Tirzepatide generally produces more weight loss, but semaglutide has more long-term safety data and a proven cardiovascular benefit.</div>

<h2>Weight loss: the numbers</h2>
<p>Let's look at what the clinical trials actually showed. It's worth noting these are averages — individual results vary significantly.</p>
<p>In the STEP 1 trial, participants on semaglutide 2.4 mg (Wegovy) lost an average of 14.9% of their body weight over 68 weeks. About a third of participants achieved 20% or greater weight loss. In the SURMOUNT-1 trial, participants on tirzepatide 15 mg lost an average of 22.5% of their body weight over 72 weeks. Over a third achieved 25% or greater weight loss.</p>
<p>That's a meaningful gap — roughly 5-7 percentage points more weight loss with tirzepatide at maximum doses. However, it's important to note that not everyone reaches the maximum dose of either medication, and real-world results tend to be somewhat lower than clinical trial results.</p>

<h2>Side effects compared</h2>
<p>Both medications cause gastrointestinal side effects, primarily nausea, diarrhea, constipation, and vomiting. However, there are some notable differences.</p>
<p>Nausea rates were approximately 44% with semaglutide versus 29% with tirzepatide in their respective trials. Some researchers believe the GIP component of tirzepatide may help mitigate GLP-1-related nausea, though this hasn't been definitively proven.</p>
<p>Both medications carry the same boxed warning about thyroid C-cell tumors (based on animal studies), and both carry risks of pancreatitis and gallbladder problems. Neither is approved for use in pregnancy.</p>

