<h2>Cost and insurance</h2>
<p>Without insurance, both medications are expensive. Ozempic runs approximately $900–$1,000 per month at list price, while Mounjaro is similar at $1,000–$1,100 per month. For weight management specifically, Wegovy lists at $1,300–$1,400 per month and Zepbound at approximately $1,000–$1,100.</p>
<p>Eli Lilly has been more aggressive on pricing for Zepbound, offering direct-to-consumer pricing through LillyDirect and a savings card that can bring costs down to $550/month for cash-pay patients. Novo Nordisk has responded with their own savings programs, but Lilly's pricing has generally been more competitive.</p>
<p>Insurance coverage varies dramatically by plan and by indication (diabetes vs. weight management). Ozempic has been on the market longer and often has better formulary placement for diabetes. Zepbound is newer but gaining coverage rapidly.</p>

<h2>Cardiovascular benefits</h2>
<p>This is where semaglutide currently has a significant advantage. The SELECT trial demonstrated that semaglutide reduced major adverse cardiovascular events by 20% in overweight and obese adults with established cardiovascular disease. This was a landmark finding that positions semaglutide as more than just a weight loss drug.</p>
<p>Tirzepatide's cardiovascular outcome trial is ongoing. Early data from diabetes trials is encouraging, but we don't yet have the definitive CV outcome data that semaglutide has. For patients where cardiovascular risk reduction is a primary concern, this may tip the scale toward semaglutide.</p>

<h2>So which should you choose?</h2>
<p>There's no universally right answer, but here's a framework to help you think through it:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Maximum weight loss is your priority</strong> — Tirzepatide (Mounjaro/Zepbound) has consistently shown greater weight loss in clinical trials.</li>
  <li><strong>You're sensitive to nausea</strong> — Tirzepatide may be better tolerated for some patients based on reported nausea rates.</li>
  <li><strong>Cardiovascular risk is a concern</strong> — Semaglutide (Ozempic/Wegovy) has proven cardiovascular benefits from the SELECT trial.</li>
  <li><strong>You want an oral option</strong> — Only semaglutide is available as a pill (Rybelsus), though it's only approved for diabetes, not weight management.</li>
  <li><strong>Cost is a major factor</strong> — Compare your specific insurance coverage for both. Lilly's direct pricing may be more favorable for Zepbound.</li>
  <li><strong>You want the longest safety track record</strong> — Semaglutide has been on the market since 2017; tirzepatide since 2022.</li>
</ul>

<div class="callout"><strong>The honest answer:</strong> Many patients may try both over time. It's increasingly common for people to start on one and switch if they don't get adequate results or have tolerability issues. The medications aren't interchangeable, so switching can sometimes unlock better outcomes.</div>

<h2>Talk to your provider</h2>
<p>Ultimately, this decision should be made with a healthcare provider who understands your full medical history, your goals, and your insurance situation. Both medications represent genuine advances in metabolic medicine — the best one is the one that works for your specific circumstances.</p>

